  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1223 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nLatest water vapor imagery and upper air analysis indicated a\nshortwave trough tracking into northwest MO. Ahead of this\nfeature, high clouds have increased west of I-57. Regional \nradar mosaics showed scattered showers over IA and northern MO, \nthough surface obs indicate most of this is virga south of I-80 \ndue to dry air in the deep sub-cloud layer. Short range guidance\nincluding the HRRR and RRFS indicate some radar returns over \nthe area late afternoon to early evening, however, forecast \nsoundings continue to show quite dry air in the lowest 10k feet \nof the atmosphere. Thus, feel anything reaching the ground will \nbe limited to sprinkles. Otherwise, mostly cloudy skies and \nseasonably warm weather will continue, with highs likely a \ncouple degrees cooler than yesterday thanks to increased cloud \ncover.\n\nWell above normal temperatures will continue through the end of\nthe week, as we remain in southwest to zonal flow, on the\nnorthern periphery of strong mid level ridging centered over \nthe southeast U.S. Highs in the upper 70s to around 80 will be \ncommon. These readings are a few degrees short of records which \nare generally in the mid 80s for late October. We'll begin to \nsee a bit of a north- south temperature gradient develop on \nThursday and Friday, as a weak front slips south into the area. \nModel consensus through this period shows moisture advection \nremains limited, keeping PoPs low and QPF light through \nSaturday with a few periods of isolated to scattered showers.\n\nMedium range model guidance shows good agreement on a\nsignificant pattern change beginning later this weekend and\npersisting early next week. A powerful upper level trough is\nforecast to dig across the Rockies and into the Plains Sunday-\nMonday. Most solutions favor an amplified, slow-moving system\ntracking across the Midwest early next week. This would allow a\nplume of rich, sub-tropical moisture to advect north. Various\nguidance shows PWATs surging above 1.8\", or near the top of\nclimatology for early November. At this range we still see\nsignificant differences on timing and location of best forcing,\nbut for now the NBM shows highest PoPs (likely category) Monday\nand Tuesday. An early look at QPF from the LREF shows around an\n80% probability of exceeding a half inch, and 60% chance of \nexceeding 1 inch, with the highest probabilities west of I-55. \nWith the increased areas of precip and cloud cover, temperatures\nshould trend down, though latest NBM still shows readings \nseveral degrees above normal through Tue.",
